<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gokstad ship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 890</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gokstad ship is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ship of about 890, found in a burial mound at Gokstad near Sandefjord in Vestfold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and excavated in 1880. About 23 metres long, built of oak with sixteen pairs of oars and a sail, it was seaworthy enough for a replica to cross the Atlantic in 1893. It held the burial of a powerful man and is displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship.jpg" alt="Gokstad ship" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karamell · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gokstadskipet1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The mound at Gokstad farm had long been called Gokstadhaugen or Kongshaugen, the king's mound, a name dismissed as folklore because other sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> bear similar names. In 1880 the sons of the farm's owner, who knew the legends, dug into the still frozen ground and uncovered the bow of a boat. Nicolay Nicolaysen, president of the Society for the Preservation of Ancient Norwegian Monuments, reached the site in February 1880, had the digging stopped, and later excavated the mound from the side. Though ploughing had lowered it to 5 metres, it still measured 50 by 43 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The ship is clinker-built, mostly of oak, and was meant for war, trade and carrying people and cargo. Each side has 16 tapered planks fixed with iron rivets above the waterline, while the lowest nine are thinner and tied to the frames with withies, which made the bottom light and flexible. The stem is carved from a single curved oak log, and a quarter rudder fastened to a block on the hull could be raised quickly in shallow water.</p><p>Its square sail of about 110 square metres may have driven it at over 12 knots, and the mast could be lowered. Tree-ring dating puts the felling of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> around 890, at the height of Norse expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and York and in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> burial chamber held the skeleton of a man of forty to fifty years, strongly built and 181 to 183 cm tall, on a bed. Twelve horses, six dogs and a peacock lay around him, and the grave also held three small boats, a tent, a sledge and riding equipment. No gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> or weapons were found, as the grave had probably been plundered in ancient times. In the 1920s Professor Anton Willem Brogger of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> identified the man as King Olaf Gudrodson of Vestfold, but this has not been proven.</p><p>The ship, the rebuilt chamber, two of the small boats and other surviving finds are shown in the Viking Ship Museum on the Bygdøy peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. The replica Viking, which sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> to the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago, was reported by its captain, Magnus Andersen, to reach about 10 or 11 knots. Later copies include the Gaia, sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gokstad ship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Gokstad ship</b> of about 890 is some 23 metres long, and a replica crossed the Atlantic in 1893.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal display reached its height in the Oseberg ship burial of 834 and the <b>Gokstad</b> burial around 900.</blockquote><a href="/state/vestfold">Vestfold</a></li></ul></section>
